###################
${bold}nf-core/hlarnaseq${normal} [https://github.com/nf-core/hlarnaseq]
Nextflow pipeline that precisely quantifies human HLA gene expression from
RNA-seq data by building a personalized reference genome per sample.
Usage and output documentation: docs/usage.md and docs/output.md in the install tree.
###################

This module exposes four commands:

    ${bold}hlarnaseq [args...]${normal}
        Runs the pipeline in the foreground with -profile singularity,sanger.
        Every argument is passed straight to 'nextflow run', so anything valid
        there is valid here. Your login session owns the head process, so use
        screen/tmux, or hlarnaseq-bsub, for a real run.

    ${bold}hlarnaseq-bsub [args...]${normal}
        Same thing, but the Nextflow head process goes to LSF via bsub. Tasks
        are then submitted by the pipeline itself (the 'sanger' profile's LSF
        executor), so this one job is small and long-lived. Logs go to
        ./hlarnaseq-run-logs/<timestamp>/. Override the queue with QUEUE=long.

    ${bold}hlarnaseq-build-image <target>${normal}
        Builds the four images that are not published to any registry:
        datatools, arcashla, hlapm, hlapm-quantify, or 'all'. Needs Docker.
        Run once per install, before the first run.

    ${bold}hlarnaseq-build-reference <kind> <dir>${normal}
        Builds a large out-of-band reference: 'arcashla' (--arcashla_reference_dir)
        or 'hlala' (--hlala_graph_dir). Both are slow and want a lot of disk and
        memory - take an interactive job first, do not use a login node.

    ${bold}hlarnaseq -v${normal}    Install path, branch, commit and pipeline version.
    ${bold}hlarnaseq -h${normal}    This text. ${bold}hlarnaseq info${normal} shows both.

${bold}A profile is always required, and you get one by default.${normal}
Nothing in this pipeline comes from your environment: every process declares its
own conda/container, so a run with no container profile fails fast rather than
silently using whatever is on PATH. Both wrappers therefore default to
-profile singularity,sanger. Pass your own -profile and it is used instead.

${bold}Getting started${normal}
The pipeline requires --rna_samples, --sample_key, --gtf and
--arcashla_reference_dir, plus one genotype source (--array_samples with
--hibag_model, or --wgs_samples with --hlala_graph_dir - they are mutually
exclusive). A params file is easier to review and re-run than a long command line:

    ${bold}hlarnaseq-bsub -params-file my_run.yml --outdir results${normal}

Add ${bold}-resume${normal} to continue a previous run in the same directory; it is not
added for you, so a plain re-run starts from the beginning.

${bold}Useful environment variables${normal}
    NXF_SINGULARITY_CACHEDIR   Where pulled images are cached. Your site may set
                               a shared default; setting this overrides it. The
                               banner each run prints says which is in use.
    QUEUE, MEM_MB, JOB_NAME    hlarnaseq-bsub head job (default queue:
                               oversubscribed, 8000 MB).
    HLARNASEQ_PROFILE          Replace the default profile pair entirely.
    DRY_RUN=1                  Print the command that would run, and stop.
